Q1: What is Java Deserialization? 
Candidate Answer: 
"Java deserialization is the process of converting a stream of bytes back into a Java object. This 
becomes dangerous when an attacker is able to tamper with serialized data and send it to the 
server for deserialization. If the application is using vulnerable libraries like Apache Commons 
Collections, it may lead to remote code execution. 
During a web application pentest, if I observe serialized Java objects in cookies, POST data, or 
API requests, I’ll test for insecure deserialization using gadget chains and tools like ysoserial. 
To mitigate this, I recommend using signed tokens, implementing object input filters in Java 9+, 
or whitelisting classes allowed for deserialization." 
